WEBVTT

00:00:00.117 --> 00:00:04.237
This is PlainSign running in a controlled
test. The extension is real;

00:00:04.657 --> 00:00:08.897
the wallet is automated and the chain
metadata is simulated. No real

00:00:08.897 --> 00:00:13.397
assets move. CatDrop is a security-training
page with deliberately risky

00:00:13.397 --> 00:00:13.977
examples.

00:00:15.238 --> 00:00:19.678
I connect the disposable test account. The
notice makes clear that this is a

00:00:19.678 --> 00:00:23.978
fictional offer. Each supported request
passes through PlainSign before

00:00:23.978 --> 00:00:25.058
it can reach the wallet.

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:30.139
Free Mint actually requests permission to
move every NFT in this collection.

00:00:30.139 --> 00:00:34.479
PlainSign marks the request as dangerous. It
shows the

00:00:34.479 --> 00:00:38.719
address that would get access. The reasons
explain why permission over the

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:42.819
whole collection is risky. This is the
permission the button's label hides.

00:00:43.986 --> 00:00:48.046
I choose Reject. The page reports error four
zero

00:00:48.046 --> 00:00:52.526
zero one. The test wallet receives no
signing or transaction request for

00:00:52.526 --> 00:00:55.986
this action. That lets me stop before
forwarding the permission.

00:00:57.115 --> 00:01:01.395
Now, Sign to verify wallet. This is a
Permit2 signature with an

00:01:01.395 --> 00:01:05.735
unlimited amount and a long expiry.
PlainSign flags the permission

00:01:05.735 --> 00:01:10.035
and shows Danger. There is no gas fee for
signing, but the signature

00:01:10.035 --> 00:01:14.055
can still allow assets to move later. I
reject it.

00:01:14.898 --> 00:01:19.098
The sign-in example gets a different
explanation. The decoded message

00:01:19.098 --> 00:01:23.558
is a login rather than an asset permission.
It receives Safe under these

00:01:23.558 --> 00:01:27.718
rules. That verdict does not tell us that
the website itself is trustworthy.

00:01:28.872 --> 00:01:32.532
The wrapped Ether deposit explains the
exchange of Ether for wrapped

00:01:32.532 --> 00:01:36.832
Ether. This controlled example also receives
Safe. I reject

00:01:36.832 --> 00:01:40.892
the request here, so no transaction is sent.
Optional simulation

00:01:40.892 --> 00:01:42.472
supports Sepolia only.

00:01:43.552 --> 00:01:47.352
Judges can compare these requests in the
sample walkthrough without a wallet

00:01:47.352 --> 00:01:51.732
or extension. It uses the same decoder and
rules, with simulated

00:01:51.732 --> 00:01:56.072
metadata clearly labelled. The repository
includes the development history

00:01:56.072 --> 00:01:57.972
and the work added during this event.
